# Historical lenses — optional learning and interface inspirations

The supplied origin narrative is retained as a creative research brief, not adopted as a proven chain of direct descent.

- Finger-joint counting can demonstrate 12 counts on four fingers and 60 through five sets. The physical method does not by itself establish who invented base 12 or base 60, or a single anatomical cause for 360 degrees.
- NIST describes Egyptian division of day and night into twelve parts: https://www.nist.gov/si-redefinition/second/second-past . Ancient seasonal hours were not necessarily equal modern clock hours. UTC scheduling remains modern; a twelve-part display is only an optional presentation.
- Pinakes supplies inspiration for author, genre, work title, provenance, and extent metadata. Do not call it an ancient Dewey system, claim its list exactly mirrors modern DDC, or label a complete set of reconstructed genres as certain without textual support. The assertion that Dewey patented the classification is not adopted.
- Clay envelopes, seals, and accounting records inspire an export manifest pointing to preserved assets. See The Met's cuneiform tablet cases: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/328902 . An export with checksums is tamper-evident only when checked against a trusted original manifest; checksums are not encryption, authentication, or immutable storage.
- The Lebombo bone's notches may be presented as an archaeological counting interpretation. A precise lunar purpose, universal priority as humanity's first abstraction, and the date “42,000 BCE” are not established by this implementation. “About 42,000 years ago” is not the same date as 42,000 BCE.

## Supported display concepts

Decimal, base-12 (A=10, B=11), and base-60 digit-group display remain independent of classification. Classification is stored as a string (for example `000.010`) in a namespace. A catalogue may carry separate Pinakes-inspired genres without replacing Dewey-inspired broad classes or the existing 101–901 shelves. Changing the display base does not compress stored assets or increase CPU speed.

Research notes remain distinguishable from verified astronomy, sourced historical claims, and game fiction.
